WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.693
Model splitting can distribute weights across devices so a larger model fits.

00:00:04.693 --> 00:00:08.137
Layer splitting assigns groups of layers to different GPUs.

00:00:08.137 --> 00:00:12.730
Other parallel methods divide operations more tightly and may require frequent communication.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:14.644
Independent placement can be simpler:

00:00:14.644 --> 00:00:19.237
one GPU serves a general model while another serves code, vision, embeddings,

00:00:19.237 --> 00:00:21.150
or a second user queue.

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:26.508
Failure and performance remain more isolated, but each workload must fit its assigned device.

00:00:26.508 --> 00:00:32.249
Serving multiple users often benefits from replicas or request routing rather than splitting every request.

00:00:32.249 --> 00:00:36.459
This can increase throughput and availability while preserving predictable single-GPU behavior.

00:00:36.459 --> 00:00:39.904
It also requires queueing, load balancing, and capacity controls.

00:00:39.904 --> 00:00:44.114
Batch workloads can process several documents, images, or prompts in parallel.

00:00:44.114 --> 00:00:47.176
Vision-and-text pipelines may assign image processing, language generation,

00:00:47.176 --> 00:00:50.238
and post-processing according to device capability and memory.

00:00:50.238 --> 00:00:54.831
Tradeoffs include motherboard and chassis constraints, power, heat, fan noise, purchase cost,

00:00:54.831 --> 00:00:58.275
idle consumption, software support, driver compatibility, monitoring, and recovery.

00:00:58.275 --> 00:01:03.251
Adding a second GPU can expose a weak power supply or airflow design.

00:01:03.251 --> 00:01:07.843
Define success numerically: required model and context, users, requests per minute, latency,

00:01:07.843 --> 00:01:10.905
throughput, memory margin, power budget, uptime, and cost.

00:01:10.905 --> 00:01:15.881
Without that baseline, multi-GPU becomes an expensive experiment with no clear pass condition.

00:01:15.881 --> 00:01:18.177
Separate memory-capacity need from compute-throughput need.

00:01:18.177 --> 00:01:24.301
Adding VRAM through splitting may allow a model to run while leaving generation limited by transfers,

00:01:24.301 --> 00:01:26.980
synchronization, or the least capable participating device.
